# MiniMax M3.1-Flash-Preview — findings by Big Pickle

- Source: MiniMax (models.dev canonical ID `minimax/MiniMax-M3.1-Flash-Preview`), announced via MiniMax Code
- Date: 2026-09-28 (UTC)
- Overview and scoring methodology: `../../model-comparison.md`
- Cross-model signed log: `../../model-findings.md`

> **SELF-EXCLUSION — zero verified public benchmark numbers for this exact model.**
> MiniMax has published no standalone M3.1 benchmark suite, and no aggregator
> (Artificial Analysis, Vals AI, BenchLM, BenchmarkList, Epoch AI, SWE-bench
> leaderboards) carries a single row for `M3.1-Flash-Preview`. Saved as
> `.excluded` per `model-report-TEMPLATE.md`; no scores are assigned, because
> every score line would read "no verified public score found" and a single
> fabricated number would poison the mean for every reader.

## Model card

- **Name:** MiniMax M3.1-Flash-Preview (canonical ID `minimax/MiniMax-M3.1-Flash-Preview`). A **preview** checkpoint, distinct from the released `MiniMax-M3` flagship (tracked separately) and from `MiniMax-M2.7`. Not an alias of either.
- **Short description:** MiniMax's newest coding-first text model, shipped as the default model inside **MiniMax Code** (their agentic coding product) and described by models.dev as a "frontier multimodal coding model with 1M context window and tunable thinking depth". Positioned for everyday software development — bug fixes, review, full feature implementation, test verification — with speed and stability prioritized over benchmark-maximal behaviour. Tunable reasoning depth (an explicit thinking-budget dial) is the headline feature.
- **Provider / access:** **MiniMax Token Plan only** — subscription coding plans at `minimax.cn` and `minimax.io` (models.dev provider IDs `minimax-cn-coding-plan` and `minimax-coding-plan`). Chat Completions. Reasoning: yes. Tool calling: yes. Temperature: yes. Structured output: **not listed** on either provider row. **No OpenRouter route, no OpenCode Zen ID, and no pay-per-token rate card found** — the model is metered through plan quota, not token billing. Promotion running 2026-09-28 to 2026-10-07 (UTC+8): double daily sign-in points plus Token Plan quota resets.
- **Release / knowledge:** announced **2026-09-27 (UTC+8)** across MiniMax's channels with the MiniMax Code launch; First Financial (relayed by Gate News, 2026-09-26) reports MiniMax launched the text model on **2026-09-23**. models.dev records `Release 2026-09-27, Updated 2026-09-27`. **Knowledge cutoff not disclosed.**
- **IDs:** `minimax/MiniMax-M3.1-Flash-Preview` (models.dev canonical, MiniMax Token Plan model ID on both `.cn` and `.io` plans). **No OpenRouter ID, no Zen ID, no Hugging Face weights repo.**
- **Context window:** **1,000,000 tokens** with a **512,000-token output limit** (models.dev, both provider rows) — verified, but from a single aggregator that carries no independent measurement of either figure.
- **Modalities:** **natively multimodal** per MiniMax's announcement and secondary reporting (image input alongside text); text output. Reasoning yes (tunable depth); tool calls yes; structured output not listed. Note the modality claim rests on announcement copy, not on a published model card — no video or audio input is claimed anywhere.
- **Pricing (as of 2026-09-28):** **no rate card published.** models.dev lists both Token Plan routes at **$0.00 / $0.00**, which reflects inclusion in a subscription rather than free per-token API access. One comparison site states plainly that a team "routing high-volume traffic to a cheap model" **cannot forecast a monthly bill for M3.1 Flash Preview because MiniMax has not released a rate card**. A launch-time sign-in-points campaign and quota resets are the only pricing signals that exist. The **cost-efficiency dimension is therefore unscoreable** for this checkpoint, independent of the benchmark gap.
- **Architecture:** **closed weights** (models.dev `Weights: Closed`) — a notable reversal from the open-weight `MiniMax-M3` and `MiniMax-M2.7`. No parameter count, no architecture family, and no technical report are published.

### Raw benchmarks found

**Every row below is "no verified public score found" for this exact model. That is the finding.**

Agent / tool use:

- Terminal-Bench 2.1 / 2.0 / 4.0: **no verified public score found**
- Tau3-Banking / Tau2-Bench: **no verified public score found**
- GDPval-AA: **no verified public score found**
- Claw-Eval / ClawProBench: **no verified public score found**
- Toolathon / MCP-Atlas / SWE Atlas Codebase QnA: **no verified public score found**
- AutomationBench / OSWorld: **no verified public score found**

Reasoning / knowledge:

- Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index: **no verified public score found.** Artificial Analysis's MiniMax provider page tracks exactly five models — M3, M2.7, M2.5, M2.1, M2 — and M3.1 is not among them.
- HLE / GPQA Diamond / CritPt / AIME / MMLU-Pro: **no verified public score found**
- AA-Omniscience (hallucination rate): **no verified public score found**
- AA-LCR / long-context retrieval: **no verified public score found**, despite the 1M window claim
- Third-party review explicitly warns: *"MiniMax has not published a standalone M3.1 benchmark suite in the current public material reviewed. **M3 results should not be presented as M3.1 results.**"*

Coding:

- SWE-bench Verified / SWE-bench Pro / SWE-bench Multilingual: **no verified public score found**
- LiveCodeBench / SciCode / Vibe Code Bench: **no verified public score found**
- MiMo-style code-agent suites (DeepSWE, MiMo Code Bench): **not applicable to this model**

Multimodal:

- MMMU-Pro / Design Arena / visual coding: **no verified public score found**, despite the "natively multimodal" positioning

**Deliberately excluded proxies (recorded so they are not mistaken for M3.1 data):**

- `MiniMax-M3` (the previous flagship, a separate entry): SWE-bench Verified 80.5%, SWE-bench Pro 59%, Terminal-Bench 2.0 66.0%, BrowseComp 83.5%, MCP Atlas 74.2%, τ²-bench 88.9%, Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index 36–45 depending on index version, $0.22 blended per 1M, ~89–100 output tokens/s, 1M context, image input, **open weights**. **None of this transfers to M3.1-Flash-Preview.** The AA index figures for M3 differ across index versions (v4.2 shows 36 on the provider page, a comparison snapshot shows 45), which is a second reason not to lean on them.
- `MiniMax-M2.7` (Intelligence Index 30, $0.22 blended): also not transferable.

### Normalized scores (1–100)

- **Not assigned — self-excluded.** Zero verified public benchmark numbers for this exact checkpoint, and no published rate card to score cost efficiency against. A preview model with no measured evidence cannot be placed on the same axis as scored entries; doing so would require inventing at least one number.
- What is *known* and can be recorded without scoring: a real, currently-serving model with 1M context, a 512K output limit, native multimodality, reasoning and tool calling, delivered through a subscription coding plan with closed weights. That is a promising capability sheet and an unmeasured model.
- Practical guidance for readers: treat M3.1-Flash-Preview as **unvalidated**. If the subscription route is cheap enough to trial, run your own evals on it — the plan quota model makes that inexpensive. Until MiniMax publishes a benchmark suite or an aggregator evaluates it, the scored `MiniMax-M3` entry is the only defensible MiniMax reference point, and the two must not be conflated.

